# Airlink v3.0 — RADIUS clients (NAS) for the GOLDEN tenant.
#
# SEED content for /var/lib/airlink-radius/clients-golden.conf — written
# once, the first time the volume lacks that file (see entrypoint.sh). After
# that the Golden app's ClientsConfService owns this file and appends/edits
# client{} blocks below the marker as NAS devices are added in its UI.
#
# EVERY client in this file must carry `virtual_server = golden`. That line
# is the entire tenant-routing mechanism: it sends the request into the
# `golden` virtual server, which talks to the `sql_golden` module
# instance, which is bound to the airlink_golden schema. A client here
# WITHOUT that line silently falls through to the Mera virtual server and
# writes Golden sessions into Mera's database.
#
# Block names are prefixed `gol_` by the app so they can never collide with a
# Mera block — both apps number their devices from id 1, so an unprefixed
# `{shortname}_{id}` would eventually produce a duplicate client name and
# FreeRADIUS would refuse to start.

# Lets the Golden backend's built-in RADIUS test-auth tool reach this
# server. The /16 is deliberate: Mera's `client docker_networks` covers
# 172.16.0.0/12, which contains this range, and FreeRADIUS resolves
# overlapping clients by longest prefix — so this more specific entry wins
# for Golden's containers while Mera's keeps matching its own.
#
# MUST match RADIUS_SECRET in the golden stack's .env.
client golden_docker {
	ipaddr = 172.30.0.0/16
	secret = "testing123"
	require_message_authenticator = yes
	virtual_server = golden
}

# Admin-managed NAS devices below this line are written/updated by the
# Golden app (app/Services/ClientsConfService.php). Manual edits will be
# overwritten the next time the matching device is saved from the frontend.
